<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/advanced/contracts">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Contract law governs <strong>promises the law will enforce</strong> — and its genius is
    refusing to enforce most of them. The elements (offer, acceptance, consideration, intention,
    capacity, legality) filter casual talk, gifts and illegal schemes out of court, reserving
    enforcement for bargains. Commerce runs on this filter.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the formation elements, and what happens when someone breaks the deal" widget="equationStep" config={contractCfg} />

  <h2>Doctrines that bend the rules fairly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Frustration / impossibility:</strong> if performance becomes impossible or the purpose destroyed (the venue burns down), the obligation can end without breach.</li>
    <li><strong>Misrepresentation and duress:</strong> agreements induced by lies, pressure or undue influence are voidable — consent must be genuine.</li>
    <li><strong>Unfair terms protection:</strong> consumer law overrides fine print — exclusion clauses face reasonableness tests, and some rights can't be signed away at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Interpretation in context:</strong> courts read contracts as business documents — the "commercially sensible" reading usually beats the literal one (the modern interpretive approach).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chitty on Contracts; Beale (ed.), Contract Law; standard common-law casebooks (Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball, 1893 — the founding fun case).</p>
</TopicPage>
